[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Current testing shows no active network fault affecting PC3. The access path, local subnet, and routed connectivity are all functioning. From PC3 we can reach its default gateway 10.3.0.1 with low latency (0% loss), the local Office B server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) with 0% loss, and the access switch management (aswb2, 10.0.0.21). Remote hosts (PC1 and PC2) can also reach PC3 successfully. PC3’s NIC is up (10G/full, link detected), and its IP configuration is correct (10.3.0.100/24, default via 10.3.0.1).

Mechanism: If the user’s report reflected an outage, it would have been caused by loss of L2 on CD_CLIENT_B2 or a missing/default gateway, which would prevent both local and external reachability. However, all L2/L3 checks now pass, so the symptoms cannot be reproduced.

Localization: Verified healthy on PC3’s interface eth0 and its segment CD_CLIENT_B2 (to aswb2), onward to the distribution/core (inter-office ping success), and to local Office B services.

Root cause: Not reproducible at the time of investigation; no misconfiguration or failure is present now, suggesting a transient condition during or immediately after maintenance.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Service is restored. If the issue reappears, first check the aswb2 port serving PC3 for admin-down/VLAN mismatch and verify the user’s cable/NIC state; otherwise open a post-maintenance review to identify any temporary port disable or spanning-tree/VLAN transition that may have caused the brief outage.
=== END ===